  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1246 AM CDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nA shortwave will dive southeast from the Northern Plains today into \nthe Mid Mississippi and Ohio Valleys Friday into Saturday. A plume \nof higher moisture (pwats of 2+ inches) currently extending from \neastern Kansas into Arkansas will spread eastward into our region \nahead of the shortwave. An initial band of showers may spread across \npart of our cwa Friday morning/afternoon but the main forcing from \nthe wave holds off until Friday evening/overnight. MLCAPE values may \nreach 2000 j/kg in parts of Missouri late Friday afternoon and \nevening, with the highest chance of this residing back to our west \nin south central and southwest parts of the state. 0-6km shear will \nalso increase to at least 25-30 kts. Thus a few stronger storms \ncan't be ruled out, mainly for portions of southeast Missouri.\n\nThe upper trough axis swings through Saturday bringing a good chance \nfor showers and storms, especially for northern and eastern parts of \nour cwa. The primary instability axis may be off to our east by \nSaturday afternoon, which would be where the main threat for \nstronger storms would reside. It's possible that it hangs up a bit \nto at least give the KY Pennyrile or southwest IN a window for some \nstronger convection, although shear doesn't look quite as good on \nSaturday compared to Friday. Again, talking low end marginal (5%) at \nbest.\n\nProbabilities for at least 0.5\" of rain Fri-Sat (via the NBM) range \nfrom 40 to 80%, highest near I-64 and in the KY Pennyrile and lowest \nacross southeast MO. LREF probabilities are about 10-20% lower. \nThere's roughly a 50% chance at picking up at least 1\" across the \nEVV-Tri State region, with lower probabilities further southwest.\n\nThe upper trough forms a cut off low Sunday into early next week, \nbut models vary on placement. The GFS shunts it further east over \nthe Appalachians by Tuesday while the ECMWF keeps it over the TN \nValley through that time. The placement will dictate how much of our \nregion hangs onto daily rain chances (which would primarily be \nfocused during the afternoon and early evening). West KY seems to \nhave the highest potential. \n\nTemperatures this weekend will be below normal (lower 80s) before \ngradually modifying through next week. Upper height rises by mid to \nlate week may push temperatures back to 90 by Thursday, with the \nLREF suggesting a 60-80% chance of that.",
